[{:seon.task/name "a-form-has-a-time-bound"
  :seon.task/plan "`seon.turn/act!` evaluates through `seon.repl/eval-string` with no per-form bound, so a model's infinite loop holds its run forever. Correct: a config key bounds a form, passed in act!; the interrupt is the named failure point `seon.intr/run` (docs/failure-points.md), and the interrupted form is recorded."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.turn-test/an-endless-form-is-interrupted-and-recorded}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-run-past-its-cap-ends"
  :seon.task/plan "Seen in the related A/B (worktree a-text-written-on-a-branch-is-embedded-there, round on-2, 2026-10-25, deepseek, :seon.config.agent/max-turns 30): the run of seon.context/an-unrun-proof-is-unknown-in-the-context recorded 29 screen evals and 36 model evals, no :seon.end fact, status :running and its agent :busy for over an hour after its last turn, with an empty stderr (runs/store-agents/main/a1.err, 0 bytes); its root answered every other request. The round's stats are notes/related-search/ab-on-2-partial.edn; the store was erased with the round (an experiment store), so what the agent was evaluating is not kept. Not known: whether the last eval hung (a form with no bound, task seon.turn/a-form-has-a-time-bound), the model call never answered (seon.model's edge, no timeout by design: a never-answered call is a recorded stall, AGENTS §9), or the loop missed its cap. The first step reproduces it with the scripted provider at the cap (a reply whose eval never returns; a call never answered) and reads the agent's state over the server while it is busy (seon_agents, the agent's :fx-pending, its last eval record), so the stall is a fact on the run (blocked, stalled) and the run ends rather than holding its agent: never a clock deciding; the fact names what it waits on."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.agent-test/a-run-waiting-past-its-cap-says-on-what}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-turn-records-what-it-saw-and-spent"
  :seon.task/plan "A turn records its `:seon.turn/basis` and `:seon.turn/split` (`seon.turn/basis!`), but not the model's raw reply, its finish_reason, model and reasoning, nor the whole split as the agent saw it (4-15 s gaps in a real run had to be traced by hand). Correct: per turn, the messages sent are derivable from the basis; the reply's finish_reason, model and reasoning, and the split context render / model round trip / evals / record / tests are recorded. The dashboard's agent page shows the recorded context, not a second producer of it."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.turn-test/a-turn-records-its-context-reply-and-time-split}} {:seon.task/name "an-outside-context-is-the-turns-context"
  :seon.task/plan "`seon.turn/context-block` (the server's read of a task for an outside worktree session) now takes the run's plan and `seon.turn/observe`'s gaps since the branch's merge base (`seon.merge/uncontracted`), as the turn loop does, so main's pre-existing contract gaps are no longer charged to the agent. It still passes turn n of max-turns 1 to `seon.turn/basis!`, so the context says \"turn 1 of 1\" while the run's env says 8. Correct: context-block passes the run's env max-turns, so the outside session reads exactly what the turn loop's model would. Replaces the second set of basis arguments; nothing new."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.turn-test/an-outside-context-names-only-the-branchs-own-gaps}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-turn-shows-its-cost-against-its-budget"
  :seon.task/plan "Found by worktree dry-run's turn profile (2026-10-23, main 8a901ac3, temporary marks, reverted) and the diagnostics timing audit (notes/timing-audit.md, main checkout): every cost that made a scripted e2e-loop turn 17.7 s against a ~100 ms target grew without any record or check seeing it. Measured spans: test-plan's reach walk 4.3 s (5 plans a turn); merge-main's three-way 4.4 s; the writer's branch-diff 2.6 s (blocking the writer); the octopus merge ~1.6 s; problems/caused 0.37 s; a fresh root's first fork queued 3.8-4.8 s behind the fork server's replay of the boot import (d/with 3,504 ms for 42,942 datoms, then load-changes! 420 ms), visible only as that fork's queue time. What exists: an eval records `:ms` and `:alloc-bytes` (`seon.repl/wrap-cost`), no ticks or CPU; a turn stores `:seon.turn/split` {:context-ms :model-ms :eval-ms :record-ms :tests-ms}, rendered nowhere; spawn, merge, branch-diff, a Flow step and the fork server's replay record no measure as a fact. Correct (AGENTS §5, docs/cost.md: work is ticks, bytes and calls, exact; wall and CPU are measured, recorded, never red), re-scoped by the owner's ruling of 2026-10-23 in docs/decisions.md (the model sees each eval's cost, stable across turns so the prompt cache holds: recorded once, shown from that fact; no reused/computed marker): (1) DONE, every eval item's record carries its ticks (`jolt.host/ticks-of`), with :ms, :alloc-bytes and :fns, with :ms, :alloc-bytes and :fns, the values the recording stores (`seon.repl/wrap-cost`, `record-tx`) and the reply shows (`seon.repl/eval-string`), never measured again. Items (2) the turn's split in the context against an estimate, and (3) the fork server's replay, a spawn, merge-main, branch-diff and the octopus merge recording datoms, ticks and ms as a fact, are not taken here: (2) the context's rendering of a cost belongs to the agent-view render, (3) stays a facts-for-the-owner task to file on its own if wanted. Proofs: an eval's record carries :ticks, and the reply item and the stored record carry the same cost values (the render's proof of a stable rendering is the agent-view render's)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.repl-test/an-evals-response-carries-its-ticks}}
 {:seon.task/name "context-render-observes-what-changed"
  :seon.task/plan "THE PLAN step 3 (the agent's turn and its context). `seon.turn/observe` called `seon.reconcile/index` on the branch's db every turn: the whole index listed from empty and settled over every task, though `seon.reconcile/done-input` reads only the agent's task's proofs and kind and the claimants of the tests that ran (notes/perf-2026-10-24.md, Next wins 1: 74% of a 115M-tick, 512 MB context render). Its cost also grew as n^1.75 with the number of tasks. Fix at the cause, with the one incremental mechanism that exists, `seon.reconcile/observe`: `seon.reconcile/index` takes the index of an earlier db and observes the later one over the datoms of the watched attributes that differ (`datahike.api/datoms-diff` per attribute range, which skips the stored subtrees both share), and `seon.turn/index!` holds the agent's last index and db in `seon.agent/state` (:index), so only the first turn lists the index. Deletes the per-turn listing; no new mechanism. Measured on a root's main of 680 tasks and 118k datoms: listing 92.0M ticks and 384 MB, observing 1.29M ticks and 10.2 MB, with equal indexes. The cost proof runs on the in-memory test store, where no node has an address and each diff walks its attribute's datoms, so it holds the linear bound and never the stored db's skip of shared subtrees (measured above, once, by hand). Not taken here: the first turn still lists the index (the fork server could hand main's index to the agents it forks, as it hands :graph)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.reconcile-test/an-index-observed-from-the-last-is-the-index-listed seon.reconcile-test/an-index-is-observed-at-the-cost-of-what-changed}}
 {:seon.task/name "the-fork-server-hands-main-s-index"
  :seon.task/plan "An agent's first turn listed the reconciler's index itself (`seon.turn/index!` with no index held: `seon.reconcile/index` of the branch's db), every agent once: on a worktree root's main of 908 tasks, 166M ticks and 700 MB with the index's deep contract, 33M and 160 MB with it shallow (the branch jolt/the-index-is-checked-once). The fork server holds main's index as it holds the reach graph: listed when it starts, observed forward at each fork over what differs (```seon.fork-server/index-at-fork```), and handed with the fork request, so the agent's state starts with :index {:db main :index ...} and the first turn observes its branch from it: 2.0M ticks and 10 MB, measured on a fresh run. The fork server pays one observe a fork (main moved by the spawn's commit) and one listing when it starts. Not this task, measured on the same root: a fresh agent's first read still costs about 126M ticks against 33M a later one; its first observe (the test service's plan, seon.problems/caused-split) costs 50M against about 8M warm with the same calls (17 for caused-split both times), and its first render about 40M more than a later one. The same calls costing 6x on the first go is cold reads: seon.db/after-fork! forgets the parent's connections, so the agent's store starts with an empty node cache and decodes every node it reads."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.procs-test/the-fork-server-step}}
 {:seon.task/name "observe-reads-are-renders"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner, 2026-10-27 (approved direction, via root): \"I want the eval system to stay rock solid and for it to be focused on executing code and instrumenting it ... Info that is derived and made for the agent or the user should go into the hiccup ... The whole web rendering only what's changed seems like an opportunity to make expensive ops mostly cheap as you only pay them once.\" What exists: seon.turn/observe (turn.clj) runs every turn: agent/prove! (a blocking test run), added-gaps, branch/unmerged, problems/caused-split, task/task, agent/merge-readiness (99% of a turn: 1.78B ticks with main moved, from seon.merge/touched-keys over main's whole diff), program/divergence and the done rule; basis! stores :observed (results, reasons, will-merge, merge) in :seon.turn/basis (derived data in the db, against the 2026-10-26 ruling), observed! holds it in agent state for my.task/status; run-tests-forked's parent waits for its child (agent.clj, read-to-exit) on the agent's one thread; test-plan reads stored-results on the branch db, where results never land (they land on main). The experiment (root's scratchpad render-not-eval.md, forms 01-16) measured them as renders: nothing moved 108 ticks, an unrelated eval 8K ticks with nothing re-run, main moving re-runs only merge-view; a render's cached output equals a fresh call. Build: (1) seon.render.ui components declaring :seon.db/db, each with main's and base's t (ctx's environment :main-t :base-t, ctx-atom's) as children: merge-view (delta-three-way; child divergence's value until datoms-diff's reads are in its read set), caused-view (caused-split), tests-view (the plan's selection, main's results at its digests, an unrun test shown running), gaps-view (added-gaps), divergence-view; placed in render-ctx's task list beside status. (2) seon.turn/observe keeps only the done rule's inputs, called directly (the judge and hand-back! call the same functions at the run's end); merge-readiness, divergence, :attention, :tests, :reds and the prove! wait leave the turn; basis! stores no :observed; observed!/input become a read of the page and my.task/status reads it. (3) each eval's commit sends one non-blocking :prove; tests-view shows its results from main. (4) (fork f) -> value, its answer a message, never a wait; run-tests-forked its caller. (5) test-plan reads results from main. Deletes: observed, :seon.turn/basis's :observed, the prove! wait in observe, merge-readiness per turn. Proofs: an unrelated eval re-runs none of these renders (ledger :hit, a frame under 10K ticks); a main move re-runs merge-view once; the cached output equals a fresh call; a turn's observe under 15M ticks with main moved (1.78B today); the judge's verdict unchanged on the e2e checks. Owner: agent view (this session), turn.clj and agent.clj's test-run parts by root's go; ctx-atom adds :main-commit and :base-commit. Phase A (this branch): (1) less tests-view and (2); the tests view waits on phase B, (3)-(5), which gives it a producer."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/needs #{}
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.render-test/the-merge-views-draw-what-the-merge-functions-answer seon.render-test/an-unrelated-change-redraws-none-of-the-merge-views}}]
